### `flex-wrap` in CSS  

The `flex-wrap` property in CSS controls whether the flex container should allow its items to wrap onto multiple lines or stay in a single line. By default, flex items try to fit in one row, but you can change this behavior using `flex-wrap`.

### **Possible Values**  

1. **`nowrap` (default)**  
   - All items are forced into a single line, even if they overflow the container.  
   - Example:  
     ```css
     .container {
       display: flex;
       flex-wrap: nowrap;
     }
     ```

2. **`wrap`**  
   - Items move to the next row if there’s not enough space.  
   - Example:  
     ```css
     .container {
       display: flex;
       flex-wrap: wrap;
     }
     ```

3. **`wrap-reverse`**  
   - Similar to `wrap`, but items wrap in the reverse direction.  
   - Example:  
     ```css
     .container {
       display: flex;
       flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;
     }
     ```

### **Example in Action**  
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <style>
        .container {
            display: flex;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            gap: 10px;
            width: 200px;
            background-color: lightgray;
        }

        .item {
            width: 80px;
            height: 50px;
            background-color: steelblue;
            color: white;
            text-align: center;
            line-height: 50px;
            font-weight: bold;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="container">
        <div class="item">1</div>
        <div class="item">2</div>
        <div class="item">3</div>
        <div class="item">4</div>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

### **How It Works**
- The container has `flex-wrap: wrap;`, so when the items exceed `200px` in width, they move to a new line.
- Try changing `wrap` to `nowrap` and see how the items stay on a single line.

